      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Neurological manifestations are frequent in patients with AIDS. Many neurological disorders have disappeared with the advent of highly active antiretroviral combination therapies. We can speculate that some of these disorders may reappear in patients under antiretroviral therapy, possibly with different clinical manifestations and at a different stage during HIV-infection. We discuss the appearance of the most common neurological complications in relation to the CD4-cell count during HIV-infection. The most frequent causes of seizures and headache in HIV-infected patients are shown. We recommend a systematic diagnostic work-up in patients with headache, starting from 3 typical clinical situations: focal signs, convulsions or altered mental status; no focal signs, CD4-cells > 200 microliters, meningism; fever and/or meningism, no focal signs. The analysis of the cerebrospinal fluid by polymerase chain reaction is now a well established diagnostic method for investigating the most common CNS-infections in AIDS-patients. Neuroimaging (by MRI or CT-scan) is an additional, useful investigation. Cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, PML, encephalitis due to herpes-viruses and neurosyphilis are discussed."},
